Explain, step by step, how a mixture-of-experts transformer routes each token to a small subset of experts, why that saves compute, and what problems appear when the expert weights do not fit in GPU memory. Then propose three practical strategies and compare their trade-offs in a short table.

A mixture-of-experts transformer is a transformer model that uses a small subset of experts to route each token to a specific expert. This approach is used to reduce the computational complexity of the model, as it allows the model to focus on the most relevant tokens for each expert. The expert weights are determined by the model's training process, and the model can adapt to new data by retraining the experts.

The main advantage of this approach is that it saves compute by reducing the number of parameters in the model. This is because the model only needs to learn the weights of the experts, rather than the weights of all the tokens in the input sequence. This can significantly reduce the computational requirements of the model, especially for long sequences.

